          "iast": "daśa varṣa sahasrāṇi",
          "devanagari": "दश वर्ष सहस्राणि",
          "meaning": "ten, years, thousand",
          "anvayaIndex": 5,
          "verseIndex": 8
        },
        {
          "itrans": "shailii sthaasyasi",
          "iast": "śailī sthāsyasi",
          "devanagari": "शैली स्थास्यसि",
          "meaning": "rock [like,] you will stay - you become a statued wastrel.",
          "anvayaIndex": 6,
          "verseIndex": 11
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1.64.13",
      "label": "1.64.13",
      "numbers": [
        13
      ],
      "devanagari": "ब्राह्मणः सुमहातेजास्तपोबलसमन्वितः |\nउद्धरिष्यति रंभे त्वां मत्क्रोधकलुषीकृताम्",
      "iast": "brāhmaṇaḥ sumahātejāḥ tapo bala samanvitaḥ |\nuddhariṣyati rambhe tvām mat krodha kaluṣī kṛtām",
      "translation": "\" 'A highly resplendent Brahman who is ascetically powerful redeems you, oh, Rambha, who are now blemished by my anger.' Vishvamitra cursed Rambha in this way.",
      "notes": [
        "This ascetically powerful Brahman-saviour of Rambha is none other than the Sage Vashishta, the brainchild of Brahma. Even though Vishvamitra is at loggerheads with Vashishta, he holds Vashishta in high respect."
      ],
      "glosses": [
        {
          "itrans": "rambhe",
          "iast": "rambhe",
          "devanagari": "रम्भे",
          "meaning": "oh, Rambha",
          "anvayaIndex": 0,
          "verseIndex": 6
        },
        {
          "itrans": "su mahaa tejaaH",
          "iast": "su mahā tejāḥ",
          "devanagari": "सु महा तेजाः",
          "meaning": "very, high, resplendent one",
          "anvayaIndex": 1,
          "verseIndex": 1
        },
        {
          "itrans": "tapaH bala sam anvitaH",
          "iast": "tapaḥ bala sam anvitaḥ",
          "devanagari": "तपः बल सं अन्वितः",
          "meaning": "ascetical, power, having",
          "anvayaIndex": 2,
          "verseIndex": null
        },
        {
          "itrans": "braahmaNaH",
          "iast": "brāhmaṇaḥ",
          "devanagari": "ब्राह्मणः",
          "meaning": "a Brahman",
          "anvayaIndex": 3,
          "verseIndex": 0
        },
        {
          "itrans": "mat krodha kaluSii kR^itaam",
          "iast": "mat krodha kaluṣī kṛtām",
          "devanagari": "मत् क्रोध कलुषी कृतां",
          "meaning": "by my, anger, blemish, made - one blemished by my anger",
          "anvayaIndex": 4,
          "verseIndex": 8
        },
        {
          "itrans": "tvaam",
          "iast": "tvām",
          "devanagari": "त्वां",
          "meaning": "you",
          "anvayaIndex": 5,
          "verseIndex": 7
        },
        {
          "itrans": "uddhariSyati",
          "iast": "uddhariṣyati",
          "devanagari": "उद्धरिष्यति",
          "meaning": "he redeems.",
          "anvayaIndex": 6,
          "verseIndex": 5
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1.64.14",
      "label": "1.64.14",
      "numbers": [
        14
      ],
      "devanagari": "एवमुक्त्वा महातेजा विश्वामित्रो महामुनिः |\nअशक्नुवन् धारयितुं कोपं संतापमागतः",
      "iast": "evam uktvā mahātejā viśvāmitro mahāmuniḥ |\naśaknuvan dhārayitum kopam santāpam āgataḥ",
      "translation": "\"Thus the great-resplendent Vishvamitra became compunctious when that great-saint could not contain his anger in uttering an angry curse.",
      "notes": [
        "Angering for trivial reasons, or on trivially inferiors ones is a demeritorious triviality in loosing ascetic power. Irrestrainable temperament will not only ruin the ascetic powers but also breeds a prolonged compunction. Manu defines<em>jita indriya</em> as one who <em>śrutvā spṛṣṭvā ca dṛṣṭvā ca bhaktvā ghrātvā ca yau naraḥ | na hṛṣyati glāyati vā sa vijñeyo jitendriyaḥ || - manu</em>'he who on hearing, touching, seeing, eating, smelling, but neither gladdens nor saddens, is a self-senses-conquered one...' The same is said in Bhagavad Gita, and the whole of <em>karma yoga</em> deals with the same subject, and it is said there: <em>tasmāt tvam indriyāṇi ādau niyamya bharatarṣabha pāpmānam prajahi hi enam jñāna vijñāna nāśanam || gīta 3-41</em>",
        "He that, being self-contained, hath vanquished doubt, / Disparting self from service, soul from works, / Enlightened and emancipate, my Prince! / Works fetter him no more! Cut then atwain / With sword of wisdom, Son of Bharata! - Sir Edwin Arnold."
      ],
      "glosses": [
        {
          "itrans": "mahaatejaaH",
          "iast": "mahātejāḥ",
          "devanagari": "महातेजाः",
          "meaning": "great-resplendent one",
          "anvayaIndex": 0,
          "verseIndex": 2
        },
        {
          "itrans": "mahaamuniH vishvaa mitraH",
          "iast": "mahāmuniḥ viśvā mitraḥ",
          "devanagari": "महामुनिः विश्वा मित्रः",
          "meaning": "great-saint, Vishvamitra",
          "anvayaIndex": 1,
          "verseIndex": null
        },
        {
          "itrans": "kopam dhaarayitum a shaknuvan",
          "iast": "kopam dhārayitum a śaknuvan",
          "devanagari": "कोपं धारयितुं अ शक्नुवन्",
          "meaning": "anger, to contain, not, a capable sage",
          "anvayaIndex": 2,
          "verseIndex": null
        },
        {
          "itrans": "evam uktvaa",
          "iast": "evam uktvā",
          "devanagari": "एवं उक्त्वा",
          "meaning": "thus, on saying - for uttering a curse angrily",
          "anvayaIndex": 3,
          "verseIndex": 0
        },
        {
          "itrans": "santaapam aagataH",
          "iast": "santāpam āgataḥ",
          "devanagari": "सन्तापं आगतः",
          "meaning": "compunction, came [to him, he became compunctious.]",
          "anvayaIndex": 4,
          "verseIndex": 8
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1.64.15",
      "label": "1.64.15",
      "numbers": [
        15
      ],
      "devanagari": "तस्य शापेन महता रम्भा शैली तदाभवत् |\nवचः श्रुत्वा च कन्दर्पो महर्षेः स च निर्गतः",
      "iast": "tasya śāpena mahatā rambhā śailī tadā abhavat |\nvacaḥ śrutvā ca kandarpo maharṣeḥ sa ca nirgataḥ",
      "translation": "\"Then Rambha has become a rocklike statue by that great curse of Vishvamitra, and having heard the words of that great-saint Love-god and Indra also took flight.",
      "notes": [],
      "glosses": [
        {
          "itrans": "tadaa",
          "iast": "tadā",
          "devanagari": "तदा",
          "meaning": "then",
          "anvayaIndex": 0,
